      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "Let owner-operated Codex agents use connected account apps",
        "body": "OpenClaw's native Codex integration currently exposes connector apps only when an installed curated plugin proves ownership of them. That is a useful default for shared agents, but it also means an owner-operated agent cannot opt into apps already connected to its Codex account when those apps do not have a packaged plugin, such as ChatGPT Meetings.\n\nThis adds a separate `accountApps.mode: \"all\"` policy. On each new native Codex thread, OpenClaw reads the complete account app inventory and snapshots every accessible app into the thread's explicit app allowlist. Inaccessible apps remain excluded, inventory failures fail closed, and packaged plugin policies still take precedence for overlapping app IDs.\n\nDestructive actions are disabled by default. Operators can choose the existing `auto` or `ask` approval behavior, and the resulting app policy is persisted with the thread so resumed and forked threads keep the same bounded connector set.\n",
        "branch": "codex/allow-all-account-apps",
